857
00:27:30,752 --> 00:27:31,556
to a million,

858
00:27:31,556 --> 00:27:33,350
the playbook is even simpler.

859
00:27:33,530 --> 00:27:34,923
Spend less than you earn.

860
00:27:35,103 --> 00:27:38,413
Invest the difference automatically in low cost,

861
00:27:38,413 --> 00:27:39,654
diversified funds.

862
00:27:39,834 --> 00:27:41,994
Get every dollar of employer match.

863
00:27:42,174 --> 00:27:43,811
Avoid high interest debt.

864
00:27:43,991 --> 00:27:46,011
And don't interrupt the process.

865
00:27:46,191 --> 00:27:47,781
That's genuinely most of it.

866
00:27:48,132 --> 00:27:50,407
Let me answer the questions people ask

867
00:27:50,407 --> 00:27:52,323
me most about a million dollars.

868
00:27:52,673 --> 00:27:53,578
Question one.

869
00:27:53,758 --> 00:27:55,651
Is a million enough to retire?

870
00:27:55,831 --> 00:27:56,982
For many people,

871
00:27:56,982 --> 00:27:59,862
combined with Social Security and a paid

872
00:27:59,862 --> 00:28:00,869
off home, yes.

873
00:28:01,049 --> 00:28:03,624
For people in very expensive cities,

874
00:28:03,624 --> 00:28:05,269
or retiring very young,

875
00:28:05,269 --> 00:28:05,984
maybe not.

876
00:28:06,164 --> 00:28:07,963
It comes back to your spending.

877
00:28:08,143 --> 00:28:09,026
Question two.

878
00:28:09,206 --> 00:28:10,982
Should I pay off my house first?

879
00:28:11,162 --> 00:28:12,694
It's a personal choice.

880
00:28:12,874 --> 00:28:15,197
A paid off house lowers the income

881
00:28:15,197 --> 00:28:17,247
you need, but it ties up money

882
00:28:17,247 --> 00:28:18,819
you can't easily spend.

883
00:28:19,169 --> 00:28:20,330
Question three.

884
00:28:20,510 --> 00:28:22,007
What if I inherit a million?

885
00:28:22,187 --> 00:28:23,163
Slow down.

886
00:28:23,343 --> 00:28:25,444
Put it somewhere safe and boring,

887
00:28:25,444 --> 00:28:27,163
pay off high interest debt,

888
00:28:27,163 --> 00:28:29,200
and talk to a fee only financial

889
00:28:29,200 --> 00:28:31,365
planner before doing anything big.

890
00:28:31,545 --> 00:28:32,462
Question four.

891
00:28:32,642 --> 00:28:34,361
Can I live off interest alone?

892
00:28:34,541 --> 00:28:37,177
If safe bonds pay around four percent,

893
00:28:37,177 --> 00:28:40,022
one million earns around forty thousand a

894
00:28:40,022 --> 00:28:41,341
year, before taxes,

895
00:28:41,341 --> 00:28:43,492
without touching the principal.

896
00:28:43,672 --> 00:28:45,454
But interest rates change,

897
00:28:45,454 --> 00:28:47,854
and inflation slowly eats away at a

898
00:28:47,854 --> 00:28:48,745
fixed income.

899
00:28:49,096 --> 00:28:50,187
Question five.

900
00:28:50,367 --> 00:28:51,809
How long does it take to save

901
00:28:51,809 --> 00:28:52,306
a million?

902
00:28:52,486 --> 00:28:54,795
It depends almost entirely on how much

903
00:28:54,795 --> 00:28:56,131
you invest each month.

904
00:28:56,311 --> 00:28:58,349
At a seven percent illustration,

905
00:28:58,349 --> 00:29:00,832
two thousand dollars a month gets there

906
00:29:00,832 --> 00:29:02,232
in about twenty years.

907
00:29:02,412 --> 00:29:05,128
One thousand a month takes around twenty

908
00:29:05,128 --> 00:29:05,536
eight.

909
00:29:05,716 --> 00:29:08,470
Five hundred a month takes around thirty

910
00:29:08,470 --> 00:29:08,746
six.

911
00:29:08,926 --> 00:29:11,248
Start earlier, and time does more of

912
00:29:11,248 --> 00:29:11,828
the work.

913
00:29:12,179 --> 00:29:13,316
Question six.

914
00:29:13,496 --> 00:29:14,959
Does a million make you happy?

915
00:29:15,139 --> 00:29:17,514
Research on income and happiness,

916
00:29:17,514 --> 00:29:20,679
including well known work by Daniel Kahneman

917
00:29:20,679 --> 00:29:21,902
and Angus Deaton,

918
00:29:21,902 --> 00:29:24,780
and later work by Matthew Killingsworth,

919
00:29:24,780 --> 00:29:27,586
suggests money is linked to well being,

920
00:29:27,586 --> 00:29:29,745
especially in removing stress.

921
00:29:29,925 --> 00:29:32,417
But the biggest gains come from escaping

922
00:29:32,417 --> 00:29:34,348
money worries, not from luxury.

923
00:29:34,528 --> 00:29:36,838
A million dollars can buy you calm.

924
00:29:37,018 --> 00:29:38,481
It can't buy you purpose.

925
00:29:38,661 --> 00:29:40,205
You have to bring that yourself.

926
00:29:40,556 --> 00:29:41,589
Question seven.

927
00:29:41,769 --> 00:29:43,216
Should I tell people I have a

928
00:29:43,216 --> 00:29:43,615
million?

929
00:29:43,795 --> 00:29:45,594
Many quiet millionaires don't.

930
00:29:45,774 --> 00:29:46,967
Not out of secrecy,

931
00:29:46,967 --> 00:29:49,414
but because it changes how people treat

932
00:29:49,414 --> 00:29:51,297
you, and it makes you a target

933
00:29:51,297 --> 00:29:53,054
for sales pitches and scams.

934
00:29:53,234 --> 00:29:54,585
Share it with your partner,

935
00:29:54,585 --> 00:29:56,387
your planner and the people who need

936
00:29:56,387 --> 00:29:57,588
to know for your estate.

937
00:29:57,768 --> 00:29:59,993
Beyond that, your net worth is your

938
00:29:59,993 --> 00:30:00,566
business.

939
00:30:00,746 --> 00:30:01,651
Question eight.

940
00:30:01,831 --> 00:30:04,014
Is a million still worth chasing?

941
00:30:04,194 --> 00:30:05,053
Absolutely.

942
00:30:05,233 --> 00:30:06,981
Just know what you're chasing:

943
00:30:06,981 --> 00:30:08,204
not a rich lifestyle,

944
00:30:08,204 --> 00:30:10,244
but a life where money stops making

945
00:30:10,244 --> 00:30:11,584
your decisions for you.

946
00:30:11,934 --> 00:30:14,128
Let's recap the fifteen truths.

947
00:30:14,308 --> 00:30:16,906
One, inflation has cut the million in

948
00:30:16,906 --> 00:30:18,592
half since the nineties.

949
00:30:18,772 --> 00:30:20,861
Two, as an income it's about forty

950
00:30:20,861 --> 00:30:22,336
thousand dollars a year.

951
00:30:22,516 --> 00:30:24,722
Three, a house alone would eat it.

952
00:30:24,902 --> 00:30:27,828
Four, location changes everything.

953
00:30:28,008 --> 00:30:30,353
Five, taxes take a slice.

954
00:30:30,533 --> 00:30:32,664
Six, fees can eat a quarter of

955
00:30:32,664 --> 00:30:33,517
your income.

956
00:30:33,697 --> 00:30:36,558
Seven, health insurance is the hidden bill

957
00:30:36,558 --> 00:30:37,784
before sixty five.

958
00:30:37,964 --> 00:30:40,483
Eight, Social Security helps a lot.

959
00:30:40,833 --> 00:30:43,236
Nine, early retirement is much harder.

960
00:30:43,416 --> 00:30:45,355
Ten, investing makes it grow.

961
00:30:45,535 --> 00:30:47,984
Eleven, spending is the real dial.

962
00:30:48,164 --> 00:30:49,906
Twelve, it buys choices.

963
00:30:50,086 --> 00:30:52,896
Thirteen, lifestyle can burn it fast.

964
00:30:53,076 --> 00:30:56,048
Fourteen, most millionaires look ordinary.

965
00:30:56,228 --> 00:30:58,932
And fifteen, a million is more reachable

966
00:30:58,932 --> 00:31:00,623
than most people believe,

967
00:31:00,623 --> 00:31:02,381
with time and consistency.

968
00:31:02,731 --> 00:31:04,159
And the three angles.

969
00:31:04,339 --> 00:31:06,755
The numbers say a million is comfortable,

970
00:31:06,755 --> 00:31:09,053
not rich, and your spending decides how

971
00:31:09,053 --> 00:31:09,761
far it goes.

972
00:31:09,941 --> 00:31:12,447
The psychology says define enough before you

973
00:31:12,447 --> 00:31:13,017
get there.

974
00:31:13,197 --> 00:31:15,930
And the playbook says keep it simple,

975
00:31:15,930 --> 00:31:17,555
low cost, diversified,

976
00:31:17,555 --> 00:31:19,327
protected, and flexible.

977
00:31:19,678 --> 00:31:21,408
And one last thought before you go.

978
00:31:21,588 --> 00:31:23,613
When people picture a million dollars,

979
00:31:23,613 --> 00:31:25,106
they picture a pile of cash,

980
00:31:25,106 --> 00:31:26,812
and what they could buy with it.

981
00:31:26,992 --> 00:31:28,926
But the people who handle money best

982
00:31:28,926 --> 00:31:30,162
picture something else.

983
00:31:30,342 --> 00:31:31,638
They picture a machine,

984
00:31:31,638 --> 00:31:34,061
quietly producing income month after month,

985
00:31:34,061 --> 00:31:34,963
year after year,

986
00:31:34,963 --> 00:31:36,936
so that their time belongs to them.

987
00:31:37,116 --> 00:31:39,345
A pile of cash gets spent.

988
00:31:39,525 --> 00:31:41,243
A machine keeps working.

989
00:31:41,423 --> 00:31:43,663
Whether your million becomes a pile or

990
00:31:43,663 --> 00:31:45,844
a machine is the most important money

991
00:31:45,844 --> 00:31:47,376
decision you'll ever make,

992
00:31:47,376 --> 00:31:49,321
and it's a decision you can start

993
00:31:49,321 --> 00:31:51,738
making today, long before you reach seven

994
00:31:51,738 --> 00:31:52,209
figures.

995
00:31:52,559 --> 00:31:54,138
So here's your homework.

996
00:31:54,318 --> 00:31:55,773
Work out your own number:

997
00:31:55,773 --> 00:31:58,044
your yearly spending times twenty five.

998
00:31:58,224 --> 00:31:59,803
Then compare it with a million.

999
00:31:59,983 --> 00:32:01,787
You might find your finish line is

1000
00:32:01,787 --> 00:32:03,060
closer than you thought.

1001
00:32:03,240 --> 00:32:05,314
Or you might find it's further away,

1002
00:32:05,314 --> 00:32:06,582
and that's useful too,

1003
00:32:06,582 --> 00:32:08,887
because now you know exactly what you're

1004
00:32:08,887 --> 00:32:09,463
aiming at.

1005
00:32:09,643 --> 00:32:12,060
Either way, write the number down somewhere

1006
00:32:12,060 --> 00:32:13,521
you'll see it every month.

1007
00:32:13,701 --> 00:32:15,299
If you want the full step by

1008
00:32:15,299 --> 00:32:17,068
step plan, it's in my playbook,

1009
00:32:17,068 --> 00:32:18,438
link in the description.

1010
00:32:18,618 --> 00:32:19,744
I'm Ryan Rover.

1011
00:32:19,924 --> 00:32:21,305
See you in the next one.
